          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.741,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Quadrilaterals"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.384,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": "So, $\\angle 1 = \\angle 2 ( = 90^{\\circ} - \\angle 3 ) .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.106,
            0.715,
            0.125
          ],
          "content": "Thus, by the AAS condition for congruence, $\\Delta  AOB  \\cong \\Delta  COD$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.125,
            0.848,
            0.162
          ],
          "content": "Hence $OA = OC$ and $OB = OD$ , since they are corresponding parts of congruent triangles. $\\mathsf { S o } , 0$ is the midpoint of AC and BD."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.162,
            0.847,
            0.198
          ],
          "content": "This shows that **the diagonals of a rectangle always intersect at their midpoints.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.198,
            0.848,
            0.236
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, to get a rectangle, the diagonals must be drawn so that they are equal and intersect at their midpoints."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.235,
            0.847,
            0.273
          ],
          "content": "When the diagonals cross at their midpoints, we say that the diagonals bisect each other. **Bisecting** a quantity means **dividing it into two equal parts.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.272,
            0.847,
            0.31
          ],
          "content": "Verify this property by constructing some rectangles and measuring their diagonals and the points of intersection."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.311,
            0.801,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": "Can the following equalities be used to establish that $\\Delta AOD \\cong \\Delta COB?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.337,
            0.412,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": "$AO = CO$ (proved above)"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.814,
            0.301,
            0.904,
            0.355
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.356,
            0.584,
            0.375
          ],
          "content": "$\\angle AOB = \\angle COD$ (vertically opposite angles)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.374,
            0.26,
            0.392
          ],
          "content": "AD = CB"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.403,
            0.761,
            0.424
          ],
          "content": "**Deduction 3— What are the angles between the diagonals?**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.433,
            0.668,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": "Let us check what quadrilateral we get if we draw the two diagonals such that their lengths are equal, they bisect each other and have an arbitrary angle, say 60°, between them as shown in the figure to the right."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.509,
            0.531,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": "Can you find all the remaining angles?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.58,
            0.339,
            0.795
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.676,
            0.434,
            0.859,
            0.646
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_3_image_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.343,
            0.656,
            0.847,
            0.715
          ],
          "content": "We can find the remaining angles between the diagonals using our understanding of vertically opposite angles and linear pairs."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.821,
            0.661,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": "In $\\Delta \\mathrm { A O B } _ { 3 }$ since $\\mathrm { O A = O B } ,$ the angles opposite them are equal, say a."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.861,
            0.428,
            0.885
          ],
          "content": "Can you find the value of a?"
